({\bf Sufficient decrease inequality})
Under 2.1~ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2 and  2.2~ass_noise:i$\sim$ ass_noise:i2, consider the sequence $\{\theta_{n}\}$ generated by AdaGrad-Norm, we have 
\begin{align}
\hat{g}(\theta_{n+1}) - \hat{g}(\theta_n) \leq -\frac{\alpha_{0}}{4}\zeta(n)+C_{\Gamma, 1}\cdot \Gamma_n  + C_{\Gamma, 2}\frac{\Gamma_n}{\sqrt{S_{n}}} + \alpha_0 \hat{X}_{n},
\end{align}
where $\hat{g}(\theta_{n}):=g(\theta_{n})+ \frac{\sigma_{0}\alpha_{0}}{2}\zeta(n)$, $\hat{X}_n = X_n + V_n$, and $V_n$ is defined in 10. The constant terms $C_{\Gamma, 1}, C_{\Gamma, 2}$ are defined in 14.

For the stopping time sequence defined in  17 and the intervals $I_{1,\tau}=[\tau_{1,T}, \tau_{3,T})$ and   $I_{i,\tau}^{'}=[\tau_{3i-1,T}, \tau_{3i,T})$, we have 
\begin{align}
& \mathbb{E}\Big[\sup_{1\le n< T}g(\theta_{n})\Big]  \\
\le&\ \overline{C}_{\Pi,0}+ C_{\Pi,1}C_{\Delta_{0}}\cdot\sum_{i=2}^{+\infty}\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[\mathbb{I}_{\tau_{3i-1,T}<\tau_{3i,T}}\big]}_{\Psi_{i,1}}+C_{\Pi,1}C_{\Gamma,1}\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\bigg(\sum_{I_{1,\tau}}+\sum_{i=2}^{+\infty}\sum_{n=I_{i,\tau}^{'}} \bigg)\mathbb{E}[\Gamma_n|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]\right]}_{\Psi_{2}}  \\&+C_{\Pi,1}C_{\Gamma,2}\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\bigg(\sum_{n=I_{1,\tau}}+\sum_{i=2}^{+\infty}\sum_{n=I_{i,\tau}^{'}} \bigg)\frac{\Gamma_{n}}{\sqrt{S_{n}}}\Bigg]}_{\Psi_{3}},
\end{align}
where $\overline{C}_{\Pi,0}:=\hat{g}(\theta_{1})+\frac{3\Delta_{0}}{2}+C_{\Pi,0}$,  $C_{\Pi,0},\ C_{\Pi,1}$ and $C_{\Delta_{0}}$ are constants defined in 66 and 71 respectively, and $C_{\Gamma,1}, C_{\Gamma,2}$ are constants defined in 3.1.

We achieve the following upper bound for $\Psi_{i,1}$ defined in 20
\begin{align*} 

& \frac{4C_{\Gamma,1}}{\Delta_0}\cdot \mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{n=\tau_{3i-2,T}}^{\tau_{3i-1,T}-1}\mathbb{E}[\Gamma_n|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]\Bigg]  + \frac{4C_{\Gamma,2}}{\Delta_0}\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{n=\tau_{3i-2,T}}^{\tau_{3i-1,T}-1}\frac{\Gamma_{n}}{\sqrt{S_{n}}}\Bigg] + \frac{4\alpha_0^{2}}{\Delta_0^{2}}\mathbb{E} \left[\sum_{n=\tau_{3i-2,T}}^{\tau_{3i-1,T}-1}\hat{X}_{n}^{2}\right].
\end{align*}

If 2.1, 2.2 hold, then for AdaGrad-Norm there exists a sufficiently large constant $\tilde{M}>0,$ such that
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\Big[\sup_{n\ge 1}g(\theta_{n})\Big]< \tilde{M}<+\infty,
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
where $\tilde{M}$  depends on the initial state of the algorithm and the constants in assumptions.

({\bf Sufficient decrease inequality})
Under 2.1~ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2 and  2.2~ass_noise:i$\sim$ ass_noise:i2, consider the sequence $\{\theta_{n}\}$ generated by AdaGrad-Norm, we have 
\begin{align}
\hat{g}(\theta_{n+1}) - \hat{g}(\theta_n) \leq -\frac{\alpha_{0}}{4}\zeta(n)+C_{\Gamma, 1}\cdot \Gamma_n  + C_{\Gamma, 2}\frac{\Gamma_n}{\sqrt{S_{n}}} + \alpha_0 \hat{X}_{n},
\end{align}
where $\hat{g}(\theta_{n}):=g(\theta_{n})+ \frac{\sigma_{0}\alpha_{0}}{2}\zeta(n)$, $\hat{X}_n = X_n + V_n$, and $V_n$ is defined in 10. The constant terms $C_{\Gamma, 1}, C_{\Gamma, 2}$ are defined in 14.

Consider the AdaGrad-Norm algorithm defined in 1. If 2.1, 2.2, 3.1 hold, then for any initial point $\theta_{1}\in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ and $S_{0}>0,$ we have
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\|\nabla g(\theta_{n})\|=0\ \ \text{a.s.}
\end{aligned}\end{equation}

(Lemma 10 of [jin2022convergence])
Suppose that $g(x)$ is differentiable and lower bounded $ g^{\ast} = \inf_{x\in \ \mathbb{R}^{d}}g(x) >-\infty$ and $\nabla g(x)$ is Lipschitz continuous with parameter $ L > 0$, then $\forall \ x\in \ \mathbb{R}^{d}$, we have
\begin{align*}
\big\|\nabla g(x)\big\|^{2}\le {2 L}\big(g(x)-g^{*}\big).
\end{align*}

If 2.1, 2.2 hold, then for AdaGrad-Norm there exists a sufficiently large constant $\tilde{M}>0,$ such that
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\Big[\sup_{n\ge 1}g(\theta_{n})\Big]< \tilde{M}<+\infty,
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
where $\tilde{M}$  depends on the initial state of the algorithm and the constants in assumptions.

Consider the AdaGrad-Norm algorithm shown in  1. If 2.1, 2.2, 3.1 hold, then for any initial point $ \theta_{1}\in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ and $S_{0}>0,$  we have
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\|\nabla g(\theta_{n})\|^{2}=0.
\end{aligned}\end{equation}

Under 2.1 ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2, 2.2 ass_noise:i, 5.2 coordinate_i_1, consider the sequence $\{\theta_{t}\}$ generated by RMSProp, we have the following sufficient decrease inequality. 
\begin{align}
\hat{g}(\theta_{t+1})-\hat{g}(\theta_t) & \le-\frac{3}{4}\sum_{i=1}^{d}\zeta_{i}(t)+\left(\frac{ L}{2}+\frac{(2\sigma_{0}+1) L^{2}}{\sqrt{v}}\right)\|\eta_{t}\circ \nabla g(\theta_{t},\xi_{t})\|^{2}+M_{t},
\end{align} 
where $M_{t}:=M_{t,1}+M_{t,2}+M_{t,3}$ is a martingale difference sequence with \( M_{t,1} \) defined in 102 and \( M_{t,2} \), \( M_{t,3} \) defined in  103.

Under 2.1 ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2, 2.2 ass_noise:i, 5.2 coordinate_i_1, we consider RMSProp with any initial point and $T \geq 1$. There exists a random variable $\zeta$ such that the following results hold
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] the random variable $0 \leq \zeta < +\infty$ a.s., and its expectation $\mathbb{E}(\zeta)$ is uniformly bounded above.
\item[(b)] $\sqrt{S_{T}} \leq (T+1)^{4} \zeta$  where $S_T = [S_{T,1}, S_{T,2}, \cdots, S_{T,d}]^T$ and each element $S_{T,i}$ is defined in D.2
\end{itemize}

Suppose that 2.1 ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2, 2.2 ass_noise:i, 5.1, 5.2, 3.1 hold. Consider RMSProp. We have 
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\|\nabla g(\theta_{n})\|=0\ \ \text{a.s.}
\end{aligned}\end{equation}

(Lemma 10 of [jin2022convergence])
Suppose that $g(x)$ is differentiable and lower bounded $ g^{\ast} = \inf_{x\in \ \mathbb{R}^{d}}g(x) >-\infty$ and $\nabla g(x)$ is Lipschitz continuous with parameter $ L > 0$, then $\forall \ x\in \ \mathbb{R}^{d}$, we have
\begin{align*}
\big\|\nabla g(x)\big\|^{2}\le {2 L}\big(g(x)-g^{*}\big).
\end{align*}

Suppose that 2.1 ass_g_poi:i$\sim$ass_g_poi:i2, 2.2 ass_noise:i, 5.1, 5.2, 3.1 hold. Consider RMSProp. We have
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\|\nabla g(\theta_{n})\|^{2}=0.
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
